⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the stale-while-error fix ships untested — the exact behavior that closed the F1 hold-dissolution finding has no regression pin**

The rework adds stale-while-error to `cachedIssueLabels`:

```js
var stale = null;
if (hit) {
    if (Date.now() - hit.at > PRIORITY_ISSUE_LABEL_TTL_MS) {
        stale = hit.data;
        delete _priorityIssueLabelCache.entries[key];
    } else {
        return hit.data;
    }
}
var labels = _fetchIssueLabels(repoInfo, issueNumber) || stale;
_priorityIssueLabelCache.entries[key] = { at: Date.now(), data: labels };
```

This line IS the F1 fix — a transient `github_get_issue` failure now serves the last known labels instead of `null`, so a human hold no longer dissolves on the first API error. But none of the three behaviors this introduces is pinned by a test:

1. expired entry + failed fetch → **stale labels served** (the hold survives);
2. fresh failed fetch re-caches the stale data under a NEW `at` (TTL restarts — stale extends through a sustained outage);
3. fresh successful read of `[]` **replaces** stale data (`[] || stale` → `[]` — a human removing the hold label propagates despite stale data).

The existing `issueErrors` fixtures only cover the no-stale path (first read fails → `null` → degrade); `grep` over `test_smGithubSource.js` finds no test for the stale branch, and `_priorityIssueLabelCache`/`cachedIssueLabels` are not exported while the test runner has no clock mock — so the path is currently untestable without exporting a seed/drop helper. AGENTS.md is explicit: *"All new JS logic must be covered by unit tests before committing."* This cache feeds three guards (the hold predicate, `priorityTier`, `_mergeStateLatchGuardFail`); a silent regression here (`|| stale` dropped, wrong `at` written) re-opens the fa#2434 incident class and CI would stay green.

Ask: export the label cache (or a `_seedIssueLabelCache`/`_dropIssueLabelCache` helper — the module already exports gh-1194 helpers for exactly this reason) and pin the three behaviors above in `test_smGithubSource.js`.

